---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="The science behind Sporty"
  description="Research-backed perspective on body fit, confidence, and lifelong motivation."
  navLabel="Primary Navigation"
>
  <main class="space-y-10">
    <section class="section-shell space-y-6">
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <p class="badge-subtle">Research summary</p>
        <h1 class="type-title text-slate-900">The science behind Sporty</h1>
      </div>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        Sporty’s recommendations draw on established research in sport science, physiology, and psychology.
      </p>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        Below you’ll find concise, evidence-based summaries explaining <strong>how body–sport fit influences performance</strong>,
        <strong>why enjoyment and competence matter for adults</strong>, and <strong>how early mastery builds motivation and confidence</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-shell science-section" id="body-fit-and-anthropometry-in-sport">
      <h2 class="section-title">Body Fit and Anthropometry in Sport</h2>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        <strong>Anthropometry</strong> refers to measurable aspects of the human body—height, limb lengths, segment ratios, muscle mass, and body composition.
      </p>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        These characteristics shape both performance potential and how “natural” or efficient an activity feels.
      </p>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">Key Insights</h3>
      <ul class="science-list">
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Mechanical advantage:</strong> Body proportions determine leverage and force transfer. Shorter limbs improve mechanical advantage in power or strength-based movements, while longer limbs enhance reach and efficiency in endurance or stroke-based sports.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Carter &amp; Heath (1990) <em>Somatotyping—Development and Applications</em></span>
            <span>Bourgois et al. (2001) <em>Journal of Sports Sciences</em></span>
            <span>Norton &amp; Olds (2001) <em>Sports Medicine</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Energy cost and efficiency:</strong> Certain body shapes reduce metabolic cost for specific movements—lighter, more linear builds favor endurance; denser or more muscular builds favor explosive tasks. The same level of effort can therefore produce very different results depending on biomechanical alignment.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Ackland et al. (2012) <em>Sports Medicine</em></span>
            <span>Sedeaud et al. (2014) <em>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</em></span>
            <span>Władysław et al. (2023) <em>Sports Medicine Open</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Selection and specialization:</strong> Talent identification and long-term athlete development models use anthropometric benchmarks to match individuals to optimal sports. Even recreationally, people self-select into activities where their body type “feels right,” which supports success and enjoyment.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Bourgois et al. (2001) <em>Journal of Sports Sciences</em></span>
            <span>Bridge et al. (2014) <em>Sports Medicine</em></span>
            <span>Malina (2010) <em>Current Sports Medicine Reports</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Psychological relevance:</strong> When body mechanics align with a sport’s demands, learning accelerates, discomfort decreases, and the feedback loop between competence and enjoyment strengthens—regardless of competitive level.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Ackland et al. (2012) <em>Sports Medicine</em></span>
            <span>Władysław et al. (2023) <em>Sports Medicine Open</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">In summary</h3>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        <strong>Body–sport fit isn’t about perfection.</strong> It is about recognising that structure interacts with physical demands so people can find sports that feel natural, reduce injury risk, and support long-term motivation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-shell science-section" id="why-enjoying-a-sport-and-feeling-good-at-it-matters-for-adults">
      <h2 class="section-title">Why Enjoying a Sport — and Feeling Good at It — Matters for Adults</h2>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        Doing a sport you <em>enjoy</em> and <em>feel competent</em> in yields measurable benefits for adult mental health, motivation, and life satisfaction.
      </p>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        Effects are modest but consistently observed across large datasets and systematic reviews.
      </p>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">Key Evidence-Based Insights</h3>
      <ul class="science-list">
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Enjoyment predicts long-term adherence:</strong> Higher enjoyment during exercise strongly predicts continued participation months or years later.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Rhodes &amp; Kates (2015) <em>Psychology of Sport and Exercise</em></span>
            <span>Teixeira et al. (2012) <em>Int J Behav Nutr Phys Act</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Perceived competence boosts enjoyment and persistence:</strong> Feeling capable increases intrinsic motivation and adherence; this “competence → enjoyment → adherence” pathway is central in Self-Determination Theory.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Ryan &amp; Deci (2017) <em>Self-Determination Theory</em></span>
            <span>Teixeira et al. (2012)</span>
            <span>Ntoumanis et al. (2021) <em>Annual Review of Psychology</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Sport participation and mental health:</strong> Regular participation correlates with lower anxiety and depression symptoms, with group or organised settings often yielding the largest benefits.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Rebar et al. (2015) <em>American Journal of Preventive Medicine</em></span>
            <span>Wu et al. (2017) <em>Journal of Affective Disorders</em></span>
            <span>Biddle et al. (2019) <em>Mental Health and Physical Activity</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Team and group formats enhance social wellbeing:</strong> Playing with others adds belonging and perceived support—factors that buffer stress and raise life satisfaction.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Eime et al. (2013) <em>Int J Behav Nutr Phys Act</em></span>
            <span>Doré et al. (2021) <em>Psychology of Sport and Exercise</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Life satisfaction and wellbeing:</strong> Large international datasets show adults engaged in sport report slightly higher life satisfaction, even after adjusting for socioeconomic factors.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Downward &amp; Rasciute (2011) <em>Journal of Economic Psychology</em></span>
            <span>Wicker &amp; Frick (2015) <em>Social Indicators Research</em></span>
            <span>Huang &amp; Humphreys (2022) <em>Journal of Behavioral and Experimental Economics</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">In Summary</h3>
      <ul class="science-summary">
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">Adults who <em>enjoy</em> their sport are far more likely to stay active.</li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">Feeling <em>competent</em> reinforces enjoyment and keeps motivation high.</li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          Regular, enjoyable participation brings modest but reliable gains in mood, stress resilience, social wellbeing, and life satisfaction.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section-shell science-section" id="early-mastery-confidence-and-motivation">
      <h2 class="section-title">Early Mastery, Confidence, and Motivation</h2>
      <p class="type-body text-slate-600">
        A strong pattern across psychological and sport-science research links early mastery experiences to higher confidence, greater enjoyment, and stronger long-term motivation.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="science-quote">
        <p class="type-lead text-slate-900">Early success → Higher perceived competence (self-efficacy) → Greater enjoyment → Stronger intrinsic motivation → Long-term persistence.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <h3 class="type-lead text-slate-900">Key Findings</h3>
      <ul class="science-list">
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Mastery builds self-efficacy:</strong> Mastery experiences are the single strongest source of confidence in sport and learning contexts.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Sheu et al. (2018) <em>Educational Psychology Review</em></span>
            <span>Pfitzner-Eden et al. (2016) <em>Psychology of Sport and Exercise</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Confidence predicts performance and persistence:</strong> Higher self-efficacy before competition correlates with better outcomes and continued participation.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Moritz et al. (2000, 2023 update) <em>Research Quarterly for Exercise and Sport</em></span>
            <span>Crane &amp; Temple (2015) <em>Journal of Sports Behavior</em></span>
            <span>Ullrich-French &amp; Smith (2009) <em>Journal of Adolescence</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Participation raises self-esteem:</strong> Physical-activity interventions produce small-to-moderate increases in global self-esteem, with longitudinal data showing persistent gains—especially in team or non-esthetic sports.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Ekeland et al. (2005) <em>BMJ</em></span>
            <span>Liu et al. (2015) <em>PLOS ONE</em></span>
            <span>Equinet et al. (2025) (prospective cohort study)</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          <strong>Theoretical foundation:</strong> Self-Determination Theory identifies competence satisfaction as one of three basic psychological needs driving sustained motivation and wellbeing.
          <div class="science-item-refs">
            <em>Sources:</em>
            <span>Teixeira et al. (2012) <em>Int J Behav Nutr Phys Act</em></span>
            <span>Deci &amp; Ryan (2017) <em>Self-Determination Theory</em></span>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section-shell science-section" id="summary">
      <h2 class="section-title">Summary</h2>
      <ul class="science-summary">
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          Physical proportions and biomechanics shape how <em>efficiently</em> and <em>comfortably</em> we perform in different sports.
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          Enjoying and feeling competent in an activity increases adherence and wellbeing.
        </li>
        <li class="type-body text-slate-600">
          Early mastery experiences — often made possible by a good body–sport fit — are the most powerful and well-validated route to stronger self-efficacy, motivation, and lifelong engagement in physical activity.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="type-small text-slate-500">
        <em>All findings summarised above derive from peer-reviewed meta-analyses, longitudinal studies, and established theoretical frameworks.
        Effect sizes are generally small-to-moderate but consistent across contexts.</em>
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
